d `overview.txt` and committed CLI reference so the second-landing child regenerates against the first's committed bytes.","cohort":["G3","G4"],"criteria":["G4"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.851,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["F10 plan: 'Regenerate packaged import help, overview help, and the CLI reference.' and testing runs 'python scripts/gen_cli_help.py --check' and 'python scripts/gen_cli_reference.py --check'.","F12 plan: 'Regenerate package help and the CLI reference through their existing generators. The Python 3.11, 3.12, and 3.13 CI cells must pass the help drift check against the same committed bytes.' and 'regenerate overview.txt'.","F12 AC: 'The help drift check passes on supported Python versions.' / 'The CLI-reference drift check passes.' — same committed artifacts F10 also regenerates.","Parent plan: 'Land each repository child as an independently reviewable Gerrit change.' — both are independent changes with no stated ordering.","container pairing: parent + 0b58-cdd8-cfed-4a07/31e0-53a5-2fe8-4a5c/65c9-aba3-c744-483a/66c6-2fe5-4850-4297/71fe-c6e9-0d52-48d8/89af-b080-1216-4229/c679-d455-550e-4c02/c97b-ac47-1349-46fd"],"finding":"F10 (c679-d455) and F12 (65c9-aba3) both regenerate the same shared generated CLI-help artifacts (`overview.txt`, the committed CLI reference, packaged help) from the same generators and both run `python scripts/gen_cli_help.py --check` and `python scripts/gen_cli_reference.py --check`, yet no ordering or rebase sequencing between the two is stated in the parent plan or either child. Because each is required to 'land independently' as its own Gerrit change, whichever merges second will regenerate against committed bytes that already changed, so its drift check can go red unless it rebases and regenerates.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fb16cb6a791cea139","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"child c679-d455-550e-4c02 (F10) and child 65c9-aba3-c744-483a (F12)","norm_id":"n020547d8ffa00d97","prio